<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Podolia</h1><p class="st-meta">1362 CE – 1434 CE (approx.) · capital Kamianets</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A frontier duchy won from the Tatars by Lithuania, ruled by the Koriatovich princes from the cliff-top fortress of Kamianets.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>, the steppe and forest land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, was under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> defeated three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> princes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blue-waters">Blue Waters</a> around 1362. He gave the land to his nephews, the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karijotas">Koriat</a>, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamianets-podilskyi">Kamianets</a> their capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Koriatovich brothers fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamianets-podilskyi">Kamianets</a> and other towns, invited settlers and granted town rights under German law. They issued their own coins and ruled a mixed Ruthenian, Armenian and Polish population in an exposed land on the edge of the steppe. They looked both to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> for support.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1393 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-koriatovich">Fyodor Koriatovich</a> and divided the duchy, with the western part passing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. After long rivalry over the region, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolian-voivodeship">Podolian Voivodeship</a> of the Polish Crown in 1434, while the east remained with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-podolia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Podolia" width="320" height="197"><figcaption>Image: Alex Tora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alex_K_Grundwald_flags_1410-06.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1362 CE – 1434 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kamianets</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yury Koriatovich, Konstantin Koriatovich, Fyodor Koriatovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox and Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ruthenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Podolia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=duchy-of-podolia" aria-label="Duchy of Podolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5296 477 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Podolia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5616 733L6352 637M5616 733L5556 562M5616 733L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M5556 562h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5616 733h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5616 733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=duchy-of-podolia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
